Drop existing tables

During development, you'll run npm run reset many times to rebuild your database from scratch. To start fresh on each run, reset.js should drop the existing tables before recreating them.

The order matters. Several of our tables reference others through foreign keys — for example, activities, trips_destinations, and trips_users all point to trips. PostgreSQL won't let you drop a table while another table still depends on it, so we have to drop the tables in the reverse order of their dependencies: the tables that reference others first, then the tables they point to. Otherwise the reset fails with a foreign key dependency error.

We'll add a single dropAllTables() function to handle this. It lists every table we're about to create in the following steps — DROP TABLE IF EXISTS safely does nothing for a table that doesn't exist yet, so it's fine to reference tables we haven't built.

In reset.js, create an asynchronous function called dropAllTables().
In the dropAllTables() function, create a new variable called dropTablesQuery that drops every table in reverse-dependency order.
Want to double-check your code? Compare what you have to this ⤵️
const dropAllTables = async () => {
  const dropTablesQuery = `
      DROP TABLE IF EXISTS trips_users;
      DROP TABLE IF EXISTS trips_destinations;
      DROP TABLE IF EXISTS activities;
      DROP TABLE IF EXISTS users;
      DROP TABLE IF EXISTS destinations;
      DROP TABLE IF EXISTS trips;
  `

  try {
    await pool.query(dropTablesQuery)
    console.log('🧹 all tables dropped successfully')
  }

  catch (err) {
    console.error('⚠️ error dropping tables', err)
  }
}
Because several of our tables depend on others through foreign keys, each step has to finish before the next one starts — otherwise a table could try to reference one that doesn't exist yet. To guarantee that order, we'll call every step from a single asynchronous resetDatabase() function and await each one, rather than firing the calls off independently.


At the bottom of the reset.js file, create an asynchronous function called resetDatabase(). Start it with an await dropAllTables() call so the database is cleared before any tables are recreated, then call resetDatabase() on the very last line of the file.

const resetDatabase = async () => {
  await dropAllTables()
}

resetDatabase()

