Step 11: Using Postman to test your endpoints
Postman is a popular API client that makes it easy for developers to create, share, test, and document APIs. This makes it a powerful tool for API development and testing, as it provides a user-friendly interface for making HTTP requests and reading responses.

Postman allows users to construct and execute different types of HTTP requests, including GET, POST, PUT, DELETE, and more. These requests can include various parameters, headers, and body data, making it a versatile tool for testing different aspects of an API.

Download and install Postman from https://www.postman.com/downloads/
In Postman, a collection is a group of saved requests that you can organize into folders.

Create a collection by clicking the Create Collection button from the Collections panel.
image
Give your collection a name (for example, "On the Fly API").
image
Now, let's create a new request to test an endpoint in your Express server.

Click the Add a request link from the Collections panel.
image
Give your request a name (for example, "GET /trips")
image
Let's configure the request.

Choose the HTTP method on the left of the URL bar (GET, POST, DELETE, etc.) that matches the endpoint you want to test.
Enter your Express server's URL followed by the endpoint you want to test. For example, if your server is running on http://localhost on port 3000, and you want to test a GET request to /trips, you would enter http://localhost:3000/trips.
If your endpoint requires headers or parameters, you can add them in the Params or Headers tabs below the URL bar.
If you're testing a POST or PATCH request, you may need to pass data in the body. You can do this in the Body tab.
image
Click the Send button to send the request.
Postman will display the server's response in the lower part of the window. Here, you can review the status code, response time, size, and the body of the response.

Repeat these steps for each endpoint you want to test. Remember to save each request to your collection for easy access in the future!

